-- Q10802: C Corporations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1059, when a corporate shareholder receives an extraordinary dividend (exceeding 10% of basis for non-preferred stock), it must reduce its stock basis by the nontaxable portion sheltered by the dividends received deduction. This prevents claiming a DRD and then recognizing a larger capital loss on later sale.
Wrong (A): Crestview is still entitled to the DRD. Section 1059 does not eliminate the deduction but requires a corresponding basis reduction.
Wrong (B): The adjustment reduces basis, not increases it. Increasing basis would give a double benefit for the amount already sheltered by the DRD.
Wrong (D): There is no mandatory disposition requirement. Section 1059 requires a basis adjustment, not a forced sale of the stock.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10802;

-- Q10804: C Corporations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 7872, a below-market loan from a corporation to a shareholder triggers a two-step recharacterization. The forgone interest ($500,000 x 4% = $20,000) is treated as a constructive dividend from Caldwell to Harris, and Harris is deemed to pay $20,000 of interest back to Caldwell.
Wrong (A): The payment is recharacterized as a dividend, not compensation, because it arises from the shareholder-corporation relationship rather than an employment arrangement.
Wrong (C): Charging below-market interest does not avoid Section 7872. The statute applies whenever interest is below the applicable federal rate, regardless of whether some interest is charged.
Wrong (D): The imputed interest is $20,000 (4% differential on $500,000), not $25,000. Harris does not recognize imputed interest income; Harris is deemed to pay interest to Caldwell.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 10804;

